### M2: a timeout and a killSignal on every pdftotext call (MPSCALC-134), four specs and one new test file
**Property of the spec edits:** in each of the four specs the only changed text is the options object of that one `execFileSync('pdftotext', ...)` call: `{ input: pdf, encoding: 'utf8' }` becomes `{ input: pdf, encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 30_000, killSignal: 'SIGKILL' }`. **No new import, no new function, no shared helper, no rename, no change to any other line of the four files** (the ticket names a fix inside the helper; four copies exist and each is fixed in place; a refactor into one file is not the ticket's scope and is NOT done). `30_000` is the drafter's default (Q-4): a valid `pdftotext` of these 1 to 16 page PDFs takes well under a second (UNMEASURED here: you time one real `pdftotext` run over a PDF you generate, Step 5, and write the number in STATUS), the value is 30 times that and a quarter of the Playwright test timeout `120_000` (`playwright.config.ts:8`), so the stuck child fails the test by its own error before Playwright's timer; `SIGKILL` because a stuck child in `D` state ignores `SIGTERM` (`SIGTERM` is the Node default). The error the call throws is `code: 'ETIMEDOUT'` (measured, FACTS 7). **Any other shape: prove it meets the property or STOP.**
**The new file `web/src/test/pdftotextTimeout.test.ts` (the committed regression; vitest; judged by the CI `web` job):** reads each `web/e2e/*.spec.ts` as text with `readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), 'e2e', name), 'utf8')` (the project precedent, `web/src/feedback/feedback.test.ts:8`; vitest runs with the working directory `web/`) and parses it with the TypeScript compiler API (`import ts from 'typescript'`, as `noBrowserPricing.ts:7` does; `typescript` is a dev dependency). The property it holds: **every call expression `execFileSync(<string literal 'pdftotext'>, ...)` in any `web/e2e/*.spec.ts` has an object-literal third argument holding BOTH a `timeout` property whose value is a numeric literal greater than 0 and less than 120000 (the Playwright test timeout) and a `killSignal` property whose value is a string literal.** At least these rows (names say the behaviour; no ticket key, no `B###`): (1) the census: exactly 4 such calls are found across exactly the 4 named spec files (a hand-written expected list of the four file names; **a fifth unbounded call in any spec makes this red by the property, not by the count**: state the property first, the count second); (2) each of the 4 calls carries `timeout` and `killSignal` (an `it.each` over the four names); (3) a **negative control inside the file**: the same checker run over a hand-written string that holds a call WITHOUT the options (and one with `timeout` but no `killSignal`) reports both (so the checker can fail without a plant). No stand-in is created by this file (a static check cannot hang). **Why the committed row is static and the behaviour is a probe (Q-2 of the drafter's report):** CI runs no Playwright (FACTS 8), so a committed row that actually spawns a stuck child would run nowhere in CI, and a vitest row that spawns one against a literal copy of the options would prove Node's `execFileSync` and not the four specs. The behaviour proof is therefore the probe of Step 6, run by you on the options each EDITED spec actually holds.
